{:edb/db-before-t 2282 :edb/db-after-t 2283 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535595 50 #inst "2026-10-09T03:13:30.237Z" 13194139535595 true] [17592186179675 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356727 13194139535595 true] [17592186179675 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356734 13194139535595 true] [17592186179676 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356741 13194139535595 true] [17592186179961 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535595 true] [17592186179961 1100 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x, y) = xi + 5j$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation $25x^{2} + y^{2} = 25$." 13194139535595 false] [17592186179961 1100 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\,i+5\\,j$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation $25{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25.$" 13194139535595 true] [17592186179961 1145 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $1$ and $5,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the double-angle formula for cosine, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(5)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }5{cos}^{2}⁡t+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={5(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =5(0+\\pi -1)-5(0-1) \\\\  & =5\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 true] [17592186179969 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535595 true] [17592186179969 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067574/assets/c71395cfe5877546509f24656ba23f0883cbd3ee8c145c10abae9600c8bd5c4a.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\,i+y\\,j$ through the curve $C$ given by $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." 13194139535595 true] [17592186179969 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3716/Source/Images/q-126940.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of the vector field $F(x, y) = xi + yj$ through the curve $C$ given by $r(t) = ti + t^{2}j$ for $t ∈ [0, 1]$, measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C$, as shown above." 13194139535595 false] [17592186179969 1145 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nIn this case, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067574/assets/e90c36e34c6ab51f15707685a22c8bd2c749b9857ca24c59631f09dfbda495c6.png)\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y={t}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =y={t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t)\\cdot (2t)-({t}^{2})\\cdot (1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 true] [17592186356727 1017 #edb/ref 17592186356728 13194139535595 true] [17592186356727 1089 "q-127146" 13194139535595 true] [17592186356727 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535595 true] [17592186356727 1100 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=(1-x)\\,i+(x-y)\\,j$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation $16{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=144.$" 13194139535595 true] [17592186356727 1113 #uuid "5cd8cc2e-7697-54b0-b7f5-b15e4ecba3ad" 13194139535595 true] [17592186356727 1145 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $3$ and $4,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =1-x=1-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =x-y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(1-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-12{cos}^{2}⁡t+9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-12{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+9{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t-12{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+9{\\int }_{0}^{0}u\\,\\text{d}u-12{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }+0-12t\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =4(0-0)-12(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =-24\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 true] [17592186356728 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356729 13194139535595 true] [17592186356728 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356730 13194139535595 true] [17592186356728 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356731 13194139535595 true] [17592186356728 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356732 13194139535595 true] [17592186356728 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356733 13194139535595 true] [17592186356728 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535595 true] [17592186356728 1041 "selection" 13194139535595 true] [17592186356728 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356732 13194139535595 true] [17592186356728 1110 #uuid "2af8769f-3ba2-53ae-ae2e-0dc77eb9f2ed" 13194139535595 true] [17592186356729 1034 "12\\pi " 13194139535595 true] [17592186356729 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535595 true] [17592186356729 1111 #uuid "de41f9d3-521e-5c37-acb1-b32048c71505" 13194139535595 true] [17592186356730 1034 "24\\pi " 13194139535595 true] [17592186356730 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535595 true] [17592186356730 1111 #uuid "f5f8d878-1cb1-593e-8008-4d3321a1f6ee" 13194139535595 true] [17592186356731 1034 "-12\\pi " 13194139535595 true] [17592186356731 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535595 true] [17592186356731 1111 #uuid "a629de3e-0c15-5ecd-8997-0aa4bb85b39f" 13194139535595 true] [17592186356732 1034 "-24\\pi " 13194139535595 true] [17592186356732 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535595 true] [17592186356732 1111 #uuid "c1dc2d90-5cff-5430-b9b3-7beda1a2c14e" 13194139535595 true] [17592186356733 1034 "0" 13194139535595 true] [17592186356733 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535595 true] [17592186356733 1111 #uuid "f0c7dc3f-bbe0-5250-b571-e5211a817e6b" 13194139535595 true] [17592186356734 1017 #edb/ref 17592186356735 13194139535595 true] [17592186356734 1089 "q-127149" 13194139535595 true] [17592186356734 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535595 true] [17592186356734 1100 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\,i-5\\,j$ across the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25.$" 13194139535595 true] [17592186356734 1113 #uuid "1a8562cf-4137-5fe9-bada-89f9787896ef" 13194139535595 true] [17592186356734 1145 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={5}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is a circle centered at the origin with the radius $5.$ It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the double-angle formula for cosine, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-5)\\cdot (-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }25{cos}^{2}⁡t-25\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =25{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1}{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =25(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =25(0+\\pi +1)-(0+1) \\\\  & =25\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 true] [17592186356735 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356736 13194139535595 true] [17592186356735 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356737 13194139535595 true] [17592186356735 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356738 13194139535595 true] [17592186356735 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356739 13194139535595 true] [17592186356735 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356740 13194139535595 true] [17592186356735 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535595 true] [17592186356735 1041 "selection" 13194139535595 true] [17592186356735 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356739 13194139535595 true] [17592186356735 1110 #uuid "8aab92dd-c322-5f14-bac1-91e7e537806f" 13194139535595 true] [17592186356736 1034 "50\\pi " 13194139535595 true] [17592186356736 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535595 true] [17592186356736 1111 #uuid "30d22da8-ab95-59ec-bab1-298b1e4434e8" 13194139535595 true] [17592186356737 1034 "-75\\pi " 13194139535595 true] [17592186356737 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535595 true] [17592186356737 1111 #uuid "0ce08f64-d5c5-5e85-9d1f-85de5491ffed" 13194139535595 true] [17592186356738 1034 "150\\pi " 13194139535595 true] [17592186356738 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535595 true] [17592186356738 1111 #uuid "6f04ffa7-bc73-5fb9-86e5-519eaabcc82d" 13194139535595 true] [17592186356739 1034 "25\\pi " 13194139535595 true] [17592186356739 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535595 true] [17592186356739 1111 #uuid "c6685bf1-76d9-5f60-a486-7d0ee5498b95" 13194139535595 true] [17592186356740 1034 "-100\\pi " 13194139535595 true] [17592186356740 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535595 true] [17592186356740 1111 #uuid "47c55117-aa8f-57ae-b7dd-fe4d7613b7f1" 13194139535595 true] [17592186356741 1017 #edb/ref 17592186356742 13194139535595 true] [17592186356741 1089 "q-127227" 13194139535595 true] [17592186356741 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535595 true] [17592186356741 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067574/assets/85b4da8c0fa35867871b6254ba20f505b7596f2c97226adb2ed01339fe4cbedf.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=5{e}^{-x}\\,i+4\\,j$ through the curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+t\\,j$ for $t\\in [1,e],$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above." 13194139535595 true] [17592186356741 1113 #uuid "8e50a0c1-1cbf-538a-8923-26f22a54c2ad" 13194139535595 true] [17592186356741 1145 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $n$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nIn this case, $n$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $T,$ so we can apply the above formula. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14067574/assets/559bbc20aa7d47eaad47a8975f80c8efb91bd03bc816357bc6047675dc755c9b.png)\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,y=t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =5{e}^{-x}=5{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}=\\frac{5}{t}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)=\\frac{1}{t}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}(\\frac{5}{t})\\cdot (1)-(4)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡t{|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 true] [17592186356742 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356743 13194139535595 true] [17592186356742 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356744 13194139535595 true] [17592186356742 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356745 13194139535595 true] [17592186356742 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356746 13194139535595 true] [17592186356742 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356747 13194139535595 true] [17592186356742 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535595 true] [17592186356742 1041 "selection" 13194139535595 true] [17592186356742 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356744 13194139535595 true] [17592186356742 1110 #uuid "f34b7049-9f94-59c4-91c2-83ced6871bfc" 13194139535595 true] [17592186356743 1034 "2" 13194139535595 true] [17592186356743 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535595 true] [17592186356743 1111 #uuid "4feae238-1930-5f22-b880-dbacc86cf5ab" 13194139535595 true] [17592186356744 1034 "1" 13194139535595 true] [17592186356744 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535595 true] [17592186356744 1111 #uuid "3431916e-550f-56bb-9751-0c37c946935a" 13194139535595 true] [17592186356745 1034 "0" 13194139535595 true] [17592186356745 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535595 true] [17592186356745 1111 #uuid "8c559e86-b46f-51d5-a994-99b60c9113d5" 13194139535595 true] [17592186356746 1034 "-1" 13194139535595 true] [17592186356746 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535595 true] [17592186356746 1111 #uuid "34a4642a-9d84-5d58-aef3-1ba884521258" 13194139535595 true] [17592186356747 1034 "-2" 13194139535595 true] [17592186356747 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535595 true] [17592186356747 111